1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,804
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:01,218 --> 00:00:02,330
Thank you for being here.

3
00:00:02,798 --> 00:00:06,366
My name is Madhu Chenna, and I'm
a senior manager at a premier

4
00:00:06,379 --> 00:00:07,984
gaming and entertainment company.

5
00:00:09,146 --> 00:00:12,897
Today, I'm excited to share a
conceptual blueprint for what I

6
00:00:12,901 --> 00:00:15,561
believe is the future of IT operations.

7
00:00:16,242 --> 00:00:18,177
It's a self-healing platform.

8
00:00:19,453 --> 00:00:23,001
We're going to explore how we can
move our teams from a state of

9
00:00:23,026 --> 00:00:28,138
constant firefighting to one of
proactive, intelligent automation.

10
00:00:29,451 --> 00:00:32,913
We'll focus on three core
pillars that make this possible.

11
00:00:33,353 --> 00:00:38,015
First, unifying telemetry to
get a single source of truth.

12
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:44,098
Second, using RAG to give our AI
enterprise-specific knowledge.

13
00:00:44,846 --> 00:00:51,033
And finally, implementing confidence-gated
AI agents to ensure every automated

14
00:00:51,069 --> 00:00:54,015
action is both safe and effective.

15
00:00:55,079 --> 00:00:59,530
My goal is to give you a practical
framework you can start applying as

16
00:00:59,530 --> 00:01:01,612
soon as you get back to your office

17
00:01:02,612 --> 00:01:06,814
Let's start with a problem that I'm sure
everyone in this room is familiar with.

18
00:01:07,621 --> 00:01:13,716
Enterprise IT is trapped in a reactive
cycle, and the primary cause is silos.

19
00:01:14,621 --> 00:01:19,242
Our monitoring tools, endpoint
management platforms, and ITSM

20
00:01:19,251 --> 00:01:22,177
systems operate in isolation today.

21
00:01:23,309 --> 00:01:25,588
First, we have a fragmented signal.

22
00:01:26,474 --> 00:01:31,022
Our telemetry, user-reported
tickets, and asset data never

23
00:01:31,028 --> 00:01:33,306
merge into a unified picture.

24
00:01:34,159 --> 00:01:39,117
We might see a performance alert in one
system and a spike in support tickets

25
00:01:39,129 --> 00:01:44,049
in another, but we are left to connect
the dots manually under pressure.

26
00:01:45,141 --> 00:01:47,886
This leads to preventable demand.

27
00:01:48,761 --> 00:01:53,174
Industry data suggests, and our own
experience validates, that up to

28
00:01:53,204 --> 00:01:58,347
forty percent of level one requests
come from issues that existing

29
00:01:58,356 --> 00:02:00,329
telemetry could have anticipated.

30
00:02:01,279 --> 00:02:05,533
A classic example is a server's
disk space slowly filling up.

31
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:10,658
The signal is there, but we only
act when it becomes a crisis.

32
00:02:12,216 --> 00:02:17,674
Finally, because of this fragmentation,
we have no autonomous action in place.

33
00:02:18,268 --> 00:02:22,930
Even for repeatable low-risk fixes
like cleaning a temp folder or

34
00:02:22,932 --> 00:02:27,503
restarting a known buggy service,
humans remain the only decision-makers.

35
00:02:28,163 --> 00:02:32,180
This keeps our best people tied
up with routine tasks instead

36
00:02:32,182 --> 00:02:34,386
of focusing on high-value work

37
00:02:35,386 --> 00:02:39,094
To solve this, I want to
introduce the Autonomous Digital

38
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:41,472
Operations Framework, or ADOF.

39
00:02:42,268 --> 00:02:44,575
This isn't a product you can buy.

40
00:02:44,746 --> 00:02:50,009
It's a conceptual reference architecture
designed to transform reactive IT

41
00:02:50,015 --> 00:02:55,285
support into a proactive, governed,
and continuously learning platform.

42
00:02:56,286 --> 00:02:59,219
ADOF is built on four key principles.

43
00:03:00,118 --> 00:03:01,492
Unified signals.

44
00:03:01,862 --> 00:03:07,543
It starts by creating a single coherent
telemetry layer across all tools.

45
00:03:07,885 --> 00:03:09,514
No more data silos.

46
00:03:11,134 --> 00:03:12,963
AI-augmented knowledge.

47
00:03:13,656 --> 00:03:19,760
This uses RAG to connect our AI agents
to a specific enterprise context,

48
00:03:20,268 --> 00:03:24,659
our runbooks, our postmortems, our
knowledge base articles, et cetera.

49
00:03:26,123 --> 00:03:27,590
Specialized agents.

50
00:03:27,836 --> 00:03:33,241
Instead of a single monolithic
AI, ADOF proposes a team of six

51
00:03:33,241 --> 00:03:38,406
specialized agents, each with a
distinct responsibility from predicting

52
00:03:38,434 --> 00:03:41,140
incidents to managing asset health.

53
00:03:42,867 --> 00:03:44,362
Confidence-gated action.

54
00:03:44,931 --> 00:03:49,583
Most critically, every
automated action is scored for

55
00:03:49,586 --> 00:03:51,750
confidence before it's executed.

56
00:03:52,453 --> 00:03:56,299
This ensures we balance the
speed of automation with the

57
00:03:56,299 --> 00:03:58,327
need for safety and governance

58
00:03:59,327 --> 00:04:05,028
Diving a bit deeper, the architecture
is a closed-loop system, which you can

59
00:04:05,028 --> 00:04:10,049
visualize as three core layers, though
it expands to six functional layers.

60
00:04:10,730 --> 00:04:13,893
At the core is a s- signal unification.

61
00:04:14,523 --> 00:04:15,626
This is a foundation.

62
00:04:16,012 --> 00:04:21,450
It aggregates and normalizes raw
telemetry from every source, endpoint,

63
00:04:21,456 --> 00:04:26,258
servers, networks, et cetera,
into a single queryable stream.

64
00:04:27,193 --> 00:04:30,363
The middle layer is agent orchestration.

65
00:04:30,702 --> 00:04:33,853
This is where the
specialized AI agents work.

66
00:04:34,433 --> 00:04:39,657
They query the unified signal, retrieve
knowledge, and propose actions.

67
00:04:40,205 --> 00:04:43,249
This layer coordinates the
workflows between them.

68
00:04:45,038 --> 00:04:47,135
The outer layer is the learning loop.

69
00:04:47,616 --> 00:04:50,313
This is what makes the system intelligent.

70
00:04:50,697 --> 00:04:56,866
It captures the outcome of every action,
whether autonomous or human-approved,

71
00:04:57,260 --> 00:05:01,904
and feeds that result back into the
system to refine future decisions.

72
00:05:02,903 --> 00:05:08,312
Together, these layers create a system
that moves from raw data to governed

73
00:05:08,337 --> 00:05:11,098
action and gets smarter over time

74
00:05:12,098 --> 00:05:15,287
Let's talk about that
foundation, signal unification.

75
00:05:16,002 --> 00:05:20,472
This is about creating one source
of truth for all operational data.

76
00:05:22,180 --> 00:05:28,096
We ingest endpoint telemetry, CPU,
memory, disk health, application

77
00:05:28,101 --> 00:05:32,532
crashes, and security patch
states from thousands of devices.

78
00:05:33,684 --> 00:05:35,720
We then integrate ITSM data.

79
00:05:36,616 --> 00:05:41,387
This provides crucial context like
incident history, SLA requirements,

80
00:05:41,413 --> 00:05:44,758
the change records associated
with the device or service.

81
00:05:46,609 --> 00:05:49,790
And we correlate this with asset metadata.

82
00:05:50,264 --> 00:05:51,596
Who owns the device?

83
00:05:51,767 --> 00:05:52,978
How critical is it?

84
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:56,097
What's, what is its lifecycle stage?

85
00:05:56,993 --> 00:06:02,842
Example, imagine our telemetry detects
that a specific model of laptop used

86
00:06:02,842 --> 00:06:07,472
by our developers is experiencing
frequent GPU driver crashes.

87
00:06:08,113 --> 00:06:13,701
A single tool might just see isolated
crashes, but by correlating this with

88
00:06:13,768 --> 00:06:20,128
asset data, which gives us who this--
who uses these laptops, and ITSM data,

89
00:06:20,254 --> 00:06:25,133
a rising number of slowness tickets from
those same users, the unified signal

90
00:06:25,136 --> 00:06:30,613
layer can flag a systemic high-impact
issue before it escalates into a

91
00:06:30,615 --> 00:06:33,170
major disruption for a critical team

92
00:06:34,170 --> 00:06:37,937
Once we have the clean data,
we need to give our agents the

93
00:06:37,938 --> 00:06:40,250
specific knowledge to act on it.

94
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:42,461
This is where the RAG comes in.

95
00:06:42,978 --> 00:06:48,619
RAG allows a large language model to
access our private institutional knowledge

96
00:06:48,709 --> 00:06:50,798
at the moment it's making a decision.

97
00:06:51,915 --> 00:06:56,214
Our knowledge sources are things
like engineering runbooks, KB

98
00:06:56,216 --> 00:07:00,089
articles, incident postmortems,
and even vendor documentation.

99
00:07:01,203 --> 00:07:05,607
When an agent detects an issue,
it performs contextual retrieval.

100
00:07:05,750 --> 00:07:10,067
It queries this knowledge base to find
the most relevant procedural context.

101
00:07:10,936 --> 00:07:16,118
As an example, it might find the
official step-by-step fix for the

102
00:07:16,118 --> 00:07:17,982
GPU drive crash I mentioned earlier.

103
00:07:18,428 --> 00:07:22,763
And with continuous ingestion,
this knowledge base is never stale.

104
00:07:23,433 --> 00:07:28,704
Every time a new resolution is found or a
new policy is written, it's automatically

105
00:07:28,755 --> 00:07:32,991
added, ensuring the agents are always
working with the latest information.

106
00:07:33,892 --> 00:07:38,872
This is how we give our AI a true
understanding of our environment

107
00:07:39,872 --> 00:07:44,438
Instead of one general purpose
AI, ADOF uses a distributed

108
00:07:44,439 --> 00:07:46,721
team of domain-specific agents.

109
00:07:47,394 --> 00:07:51,538
This makes the system more manageable,
transparent, and effective.

110
00:07:52,610 --> 00:07:57,178
Incident prediction detects precursor
signals, like rising memory issue

111
00:07:57,178 --> 00:08:01,558
on a server cluster, to flag issues
before tickets are even created.

112
00:08:03,156 --> 00:08:08,452
Asset health agent monitors device life
cycles and predicts hardware failures.

113
00:08:09,111 --> 00:08:13,452
For example, it can analyze a battery
health across our laptop fleet and

114
00:08:13,452 --> 00:08:15,976
predict when a device is likely to fail.

115
00:08:17,412 --> 00:08:19,688
Auto-remediation is the action arm.

116
00:08:20,169 --> 00:08:24,832
It executes approved low-risk
fixes, like restarting a crashed

117
00:08:24,842 --> 00:08:27,771
service or clearing a full cache.

118
00:08:29,087 --> 00:08:34,327
Procurement intelligence uses signals from
asset health agent to trigger replacement

119
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:40,815
or refresh workflows, ensuring a developer
gets a laptop before the old one dies User

120
00:08:40,816 --> 00:08:45,857
experience agent correlates user-facing
issues like slow application load times

121
00:08:46,209 --> 00:08:51,199
with backend infrastructure events to
pinpoint the root cause of UX degradation.

122
00:08:52,274 --> 00:08:56,161
Finally, compliance and
audit agent is our governor.

123
00:08:56,365 --> 00:09:01,716
It validates every proposed autonomous
action against internal security and

124
00:09:01,726 --> 00:09:04,799
IT policies, acting as a final check

125
00:09:05,799 --> 00:09:09,115
Now, this is the most important
design decision in the entire

126
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:11,133
framework, the confidence gate.

127
00:09:12,081 --> 00:09:15,828
Before any agent can execute
an action, it must calculate

128
00:09:15,864 --> 00:09:17,491
a composite confidence score.

129
00:09:18,582 --> 00:09:22,332
This score determines if the
platform acts autonomously or

130
00:09:22,416 --> 00:09:24,551
routes the issue for human approval.

131
00:09:25,668 --> 00:09:30,609
The score is calculated across
three dimensions: enterprise health

132
00:09:30,609 --> 00:09:35,022
score, business impact score,
and automation confidence score.

133
00:09:36,250 --> 00:09:37,637
Enterprise health score.

134
00:09:38,167 --> 00:09:41,804
What is the current stability
of our overall environment?

135
00:09:42,245 --> 00:09:46,346
If our core network is already
unstable, we probably don't

136
00:09:46,346 --> 00:09:48,203
want to make automated changes.

137
00:09:48,744 --> 00:09:51,318
This assesses the blast radius.

138
00:09:52,718 --> 00:09:53,952
Business impact score.

139
00:09:54,414 --> 00:09:58,895
How critical are the affected
users, services, and SLAs?

140
00:09:59,295 --> 00:10:03,690
An automated fix for a low-priority
internal wiki is very different

141
00:10:03,698 --> 00:10:08,155
from one on a production, game
authentication server as an example.

142
00:10:09,402 --> 00:10:10,973
Automation confidence score.

143
00:10:11,094 --> 00:10:15,299
What is the historical success
rate for this exact action pattern?

144
00:10:15,907 --> 00:10:21,457
If an automated fix has worked a hundred
times before, its confidence is very high.

145
00:10:22,098 --> 00:10:25,403
If it has failed before,
that score is lower.

146
00:10:26,505 --> 00:10:28,742
Based on this, we set thresholds.

147
00:10:29,110 --> 00:10:34,644
For example, any score above
eighty-five percent might proceed

148
00:10:34,646 --> 00:10:35,917
with autonomous execution.

149
00:10:36,476 --> 00:10:40,560
Anything between sixty and eighty-five
becomes a recommendation for a

150
00:10:40,561 --> 00:10:44,808
human to approve with a single
click, and anything below sixty

151
00:10:44,855 --> 00:10:46,854
is routed for full manual review

152
00:10:47,854 --> 00:10:48,759
Let's make this real.

153
00:10:49,394 --> 00:10:52,062
Above eighty-five percent means execute.

154
00:10:52,378 --> 00:10:54,674
Below sixty percent
means route to a human.

155
00:10:55,357 --> 00:10:59,223
That middle ground between sixty
and eighty-five percent is where

156
00:10:59,272 --> 00:11:01,349
assisted recommendation shines.

157
00:11:01,962 --> 00:11:04,012
The system doesn't just create a ticket.

158
00:11:04,512 --> 00:11:08,820
It creates a ticket that says,
"Here is a problem, here is a po-

159
00:11:09,156 --> 00:11:12,799
probable root cause, and here is
a recommendation-- recommended fix

160
00:11:12,824 --> 00:11:14,789
with one-click approval button."

161
00:11:15,506 --> 00:11:20,521
This confidence gate is what allows
us to have both autonomous speed and

162
00:11:20,537 --> 00:11:23,117
governed safety in same platform.

163
00:11:23,712 --> 00:11:26,756
It's a mechanism that builds
human trust in the system

164
00:11:27,756 --> 00:11:30,045
The system is designed to improve.

165
00:11:30,466 --> 00:11:34,336
Every action ADOF takes
feeds back into the system.

166
00:11:34,913 --> 00:11:37,875
When an action is taken,
the platform observes the

167
00:11:37,876 --> 00:11:39,512
outcome and scores the result.

168
00:11:40,050 --> 00:11:41,154
Did the fix work?

169
00:11:41,233 --> 00:11:42,981
Did the user's issue go away?

170
00:11:43,682 --> 00:11:47,974
Successful remediations reinforce and
increase the automation confidence

171
00:11:47,981 --> 00:11:52,432
score for that pattern, making it more
likely to be automated in the future.

172
00:11:53,740 --> 00:11:58,004
Crucially, human overrides are
captured as labeled training signals.

173
00:11:58,182 --> 00:12:01,927
If a human rejects a recommendation,
the system learns from that decision.

174
00:12:02,069 --> 00:12:06,117
It asks why and uses that
answer to improve its decision.

175
00:12:07,719 --> 00:12:13,300
The system also includes drift
detection to recognize when a previously

176
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:17,573
successful fix starts failing,
preventing model degradation over time.

177
00:12:18,082 --> 00:12:23,245
This means the system doesn't just
get faster at handling incidents, it

178
00:12:23,247 --> 00:12:25,737
gets smarter with every single one

179
00:12:26,737 --> 00:12:28,881
Implementing this isn't a big bang.

180
00:12:29,345 --> 00:12:30,073
It's a journey.

181
00:12:30,471 --> 00:12:34,379
We have defined a five-level
maturity model to provide a clear

182
00:12:34,401 --> 00:12:36,755
governed path for expanding autonomy.

183
00:12:38,471 --> 00:12:39,823
Level one is observe.

184
00:12:40,417 --> 00:12:42,725
Agents simply monitor and alert.

185
00:12:43,206 --> 00:12:46,541
No automated actions
are taken in this level.

186
00:12:47,076 --> 00:12:50,418
This is about building the
signal unification layer.

187
00:12:51,632 --> 00:12:53,109
Level two is recommend.

188
00:12:53,565 --> 00:12:58,603
Agents surface fix options, but humans
must approve every single action.

189
00:12:59,810 --> 00:13:02,434
Then comes level three,
assisted execution.

190
00:13:03,161 --> 00:13:05,938
This is where the
confidence gate kicks in.

191
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:12,185
Low-risk, high-confidence actions are
executed automatically for the first time.

192
00:13:13,213 --> 00:13:14,739
Supervised autonomy.

193
00:13:15,090 --> 00:13:20,598
Here we have a broad automated execution
with humans only reviewing the exceptions.

194
00:13:22,494 --> 00:13:24,414
Level five is full autonomy.

195
00:13:25,002 --> 00:13:30,140
The system moves to a state of
self-healing with continuous va-- policy

196
00:13:30,140 --> 00:13:34,827
validation and audit logging, and humans
focused on oversight and evolution.

197
00:13:35,546 --> 00:13:39,261
This staged approach allows
teams to build trust and expand

198
00:13:39,269 --> 00:13:42,226
capability at their own pace.

199
00:13:43,226 --> 00:13:44,584
So what is the business outcome?

200
00:13:45,167 --> 00:13:48,917
Based on our modeling, these are
illustrative targets we aim for.

201
00:13:49,524 --> 00:13:53,309
I want to stress these are proposals
for future validation as actual

202
00:13:53,309 --> 00:13:55,515
outcomes will depend on the environment.

203
00:13:56,247 --> 00:14:01,349
A forty percent reduction in level
one support requests by predicting and

204
00:14:01,349 --> 00:14:04,437
resolving issues before users notice them.

205
00:14:05,497 --> 00:14:08,637
An eighty-five percent confidence
threshold as our gate for

206
00:14:08,637 --> 00:14:12,644
fully autonomous remediation,
ensuring a high degree of safety.

207
00:14:13,655 --> 00:14:19,161
A team of six specialized agents
working in concert, and a journey

208
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:23,260
through five maturity levels to
ensure governance and build trust.

209
00:14:24,216 --> 00:14:29,721
This is about turning IT operations from
a cost center into a strategic enabler

210
00:14:30,101 --> 00:14:31,965
that protects employee productivity

211
00:14:32,965 --> 00:14:38,370
ADOF is a big vision, but you can start
applying its core design patterns today.

212
00:14:38,963 --> 00:14:41,260
Unify before you automate.

213
00:14:42,057 --> 00:14:44,486
Don't build automation on siloed data.

214
00:14:45,389 --> 00:14:48,353
Signal correlation is the
absolute prerequisite.

215
00:14:48,583 --> 00:14:53,811
Start by bringing your telemetry,
ITSM, and asset data into one place.

216
00:14:55,065 --> 00:14:57,295
Gate on confidence, not rules.

217
00:14:57,799 --> 00:15:01,348
Static rule-based automation
is brittle and will break.

218
00:15:01,482 --> 00:15:05,582
A composite confidence score
is adaptive and resilient.

219
00:15:06,566 --> 00:15:08,236
Specialize your agents.

220
00:15:08,344 --> 00:15:12,963
Domain-specific agents will always
outperform general-purpose ones

221
00:15:13,121 --> 00:15:14,474
in a production environment.

222
00:15:14,833 --> 00:15:18,918
Start with one domain, like asset
health, and build from there.

223
00:15:19,922 --> 00:15:21,516
Build a feedback loop first.

224
00:15:21,827 --> 00:15:27,161
If you don't capture the outcomes of your
actions, your systems cannot improve.

225
00:15:27,659 --> 00:15:32,273
Make outcome capture a
structural, non-negotiable part

226
00:15:32,280 --> 00:15:33,942
of your process from day one

227
00:15:34,942 --> 00:15:39,900
In closing, the path to self-healing
operations is an incremental one.

228
00:15:40,455 --> 00:15:43,540
ADOF is a reference architecture,
not a finished product.

229
00:15:44,035 --> 00:15:48,156
It's a blueprint to help teams move
from reactive to proactive operations

230
00:15:48,232 --> 00:15:52,852
at their own pace with human trust
built in from the very start.

231
00:15:53,588 --> 00:15:55,886
The journey begins with
three simple steps.

232
00:15:56,102 --> 00:16:01,295
Start with signal unification,
gate every action on confidence,

233
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:04,265
and expand autonomy incrementally

234
00:16:05,265 --> 00:16:07,554
That brings us to the
end of this presentation.

235
00:16:07,721 --> 00:16:08,795
Thank you for your time today.

236
00:16:08,826 --> 00:16:12,980
I hope this has provided you with a
valuable framework for thinking about

237
00:16:12,986 --> 00:16:15,560
the future of your own IT operations.

238
00:16:16,504 --> 00:16:17,067
Thanks again.
